            "In the past three decades, methods known generically as everyday experience methods have matured from promising innovations into standard, widely used tools, referring not to a specific instrument or procedure but to a paradigm for studying social psychological phenomena as they occur in the ebb and flow of everyday life, to 'capture life as it is lived' (Bolger et al., 2003).",
