            "Recency: the more recent an event, the better it is recalled and the more likely it is to influence retrospection, as shown by Bernard et al. (1984), where fewer than half of informant-recalled social network contacts were remembered correctly but more recent events were recalled better than less recent ones.",
            "Accessibility: more salient events, in terms of intensity, unusualness, or personal significance, tend to be more influential, so that summary ratings of daily or weekly emotion echo moments of peak intensity more than they reflect average levels (Neubauer et al., 2020).",
            "Sense-making: compared to contemporaneous reports, remembered experiences tend to be interpreted in the context of beliefs about those experiences, as when women's recollections of their menstrual symptoms better resemble their general beliefs about such symptoms than their daily symptom reports (McFarland et al., 1989).",
